※ pl4g-test: compile=error diag=4547

※ A type is not a value, so it is lifted out of the program the way it is
※ everywhere the compiler is asked about one.  Without the marks the name would
※ be read as a value's -- a type's name and a value's name being both
※ identifiers -- and which was meant would depend on what the name turned out
※ to be, which is the thing the marks are there to stop.

@[startup]
fn main() → u6:
    let n: u32 = 300u32
    let small: u8?⎕narrowing = ⎕narrow(n, u8)
    0u6
